// WRONG-CODE BUG, reproduced — `x in CONST_ARRAY` mis-compiles when the module
// constant is declared AFTER the function that uses it.
//
// Diagnostic-only (`_diag_` prefix): no `.snapshot` sidecars, so the snippet
// harness skips it. Kept as a committed reproduction — running it traps, it does
// not print.
//
// Symptom, both backends, no diagnostic at compile time:
//   vader run   → `vader vm: local.set on empty stack at pc=7 in 'app$main'`
//   vader build → `vader: reached unreachable at unreachable return in main$hit`
//
// The boundary, measured:
//   * a SCALAR const used before its declaration     → fine
//   * INDEXING the same array const (`NAMES[0]`)     → fine
//   * the same array const declared BEFORE the use   → fine
//   * `in` against it, declared after the use        → BROKEN
//
// So it is specific to the `in` operator's lowering when its right operand is a
// forward-referenced module-level array constant. Top-level declarations are
// order-independent everywhere else in the language, which is what makes this a
// bug rather than a rule.
//
// MECHANISM, traced — two separable defects:
//
//  1. `lower_binary.vader:91` lowers `x in coll` by resolving `Contains` on
//     `ctx.types.expr_type(binary.right)`. The typechecker DOES type the constant
//     (this snippet's `typecheck.snapshot` records `ConstDecl NAMES :: string[]`,
//     with no diagnostics) — it is the USE SITE's entry that is missing, so the
//     lowerer reads `TY_UNRESOLVED`, finds no `Contains` impl, and falls through.
//     Same shape as the known `expr_types` hazard: the table is keyed on node id
//     and the downstream reader degrades silently
//     (`expr_types[expr.id] ?? return TY_UNRESOLVED`).
//  2. That fall-through is `lower_binary.vader:119`:
//     `stub(binary.span, type, "\`in\` / \`not in\` lowering deferred")`, which
//     emits `unreachable` in a VALUE position. So an unhandled `in` shape becomes
//     a runtime trap with NO compile-time signal — visible in this snippet's
//     `lower.snapshot`. Even once (1) is fixed, any other unhandled shape would
//     fail the same silent way; an `M5xxx` here would turn wrong code into a
//     diagnostic.
//
// Found by dogfooding the build driver: a `build.vader` whose helper tables sat
// at the bottom of the file (`tests/hook_fixtures/json_derive/`), which is where
// this file's ordering comes from rather than from a contrived shape.

module "snippet"

import "std/io"

// Uses `NUMERIC` and `NAMES`, both declared below.
kind :: fn(name: string) -> string {
    if name in NUMERIC {
        return "number"
    }
    if name in NAMES {
        return "name"
    }
    return "other"
}

// These three work, and are kept so the repro shows what the bug is NOT.
first_numeric :: fn() -> string = NUMERIC[0]
limit         :: fn() -> i32 = LIMIT
LIMIT         :: 42

NUMERIC :: ["i32", "f64"]
NAMES   :: ["alice", "bob"]

main :: fn() -> i32 {
    // The working cases first, so a partial run shows how far it got.
    println("${first_numeric()} ${limit()}")
    // Traps here.
    println("${kind("i32")} ${kind("alice")} ${kind("zzz")}")
    return 0
}
